\section{Executive Summary}
\label{sec:executive-summary}

This document provides a comprehensive technical analysis of the NestUp BD platform, a modern property rental solution designed specifically for the Bangladeshi market. The platform serves students and professionals seeking accommodation through an intuitive web-based interface that facilitates property listing, searching, booking, and secure payment processing.

\vspace{0.5cm}

\section{Project Overview}
\label{sec:project-overview}

NestUp BD is a comprehensive property rental platform designed specifically for students and professionals in Bangladesh. The application facilitates property listing, searching, booking, and payment processing with a modern web-based interface.

\subsection{Technology Stack}
\label{subsec:technology-stack}

The platform is built using modern web technologies to ensure scalability, maintainability, and optimal performance:

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{@{}ll@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Component} & \textbf{Technology} \\
\midrule
Frontend Framework & React.js with Vite build tool \\
Backend Framework & Node.js with Express.js \\
Database & MongoDB with Mongoose ODM \\
Authentication & JWT (JSON Web Tokens) \\
File Upload & Multer middleware \\
Styling & CSS with custom variables and responsive design \\
Development Tools & ESLint, Prettier, Nodemon \\
Version Control & Git with GitHub \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Technology Stack Overview}
\label{tab:tech-stack}
\end{table}

\subsection{Key Features}
\label{subsec:key-features}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Multi-tenant Architecture}: Support for property owners and tenants
\item \textbf{Advanced Search}: Location-based filtering with multiple criteria
\item \textbf{Secure Payments}: Multiple payment methods with transaction tracking
\item \textbf{Real-time Updates}: Live availability and booking status
\item \textbf{Mobile Responsive}: Optimized for all device types
\item \textbf{Admin Dashboard}: Comprehensive management interface
\end{itemize}

\section{Project Structure}
\label{sec:project-structure}

The project follows a well-organized modular structure that separates concerns between frontend and backend components:

\begin{lstlisting}[caption=Project Directory Structure, label=lst:project-structure]
NestUp BD/
├── Client/                          # Frontend React application
│   ├── src/
│   │   ├── components/              # Reusable UI components
│   │   ├── pages/                   # Main application pages
│   │   ├── context/                 # React Context for state management
│   │   ├── utils/                   # Utility functions
│   │   └── assets/                  # Static assets
│   ├── public/                      # Public static files
│   ├── package.json                 # Frontend dependencies
│   └── vite.config.js              # Vite configuration
├── server/                          # Backend Node.js application
│   ├── models/                      # MongoDB schemas and models
│   ├── routes/                      # API route handlers
│   ├── middleware/                  # Custom middleware functions
│   ├── uploads/                     # File upload storage
│   ├── server.js                    # Main server entry point
│   ├── package.json                 # Backend dependencies
│   └── .env                         # Environment variables
├── Docs/                            # Documentation files
└── test-payment.js                  # Payment testing script
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{Architecture Principles}
\label{subsec:architecture-principles}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Separation of Concerns}: Clear distinction between frontend and backend responsibilities
\item \textbf{Modular Design}: Components are organized into logical modules for maintainability
\item \textbf{RESTful API}: Standard HTTP methods and status codes for API communication
\item \textbf{Security First}: JWT authentication and input validation at all levels
\item \textbf{Scalable Structure}: Organized codebase that supports future enhancements
\end{itemize}

\section{Backend Architecture}
\label{sec:backend-architecture}

\subsection{Server Entry Point}
\label{subsec:server-entry-point}

\textbf{File}: \file{server/server.js}

\textbf{Purpose}: Main application server that initializes the Express app, connects to MongoDB, sets up middleware, and starts the server.

\subsubsection{Key Functionalities}
\begin{itemize}
\item Express server initialization and configuration
\item MongoDB connection using Mongoose ODM
\item CORS configuration for cross-origin requests
\item Cookie parser for JWT token handling
\item Static file serving for uploaded content
\item API route mounting and error handling
\item Server startup on port 3000
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Server Initialization Flow}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Load environment variables from \file{.env} file
\item Create Express application instance
\item Configure middleware (CORS, JSON parser, cookie parser)
\item Establish MongoDB database connection
\item Mount API routes and middleware
\item Start server and listen on specified port
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Database Models}
\label{subsec:database-models}

\textbf{Location}: \folder{server/models/}

The application uses MongoDB with Mongoose ODM for data modeling. Each model defines the schema, validation rules, and business logic for different entities.

\subsubsection{User Model}
\label{subsubsec:user-model}

\textbf{File}: \file{User.js}

\textbf{Purpose}: Defines the user schema for authentication and profile management.

\paragraph{Schema Fields}
\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Field} & \textbf{Type} & \textbf{Constraints} \\
\midrule
name & String & Required, trimmed, min 2 chars \\
email & String & Required, unique, lowercase, email validation \\
password & String & Required, min 6 chars, hashed \\
role & Enum & ['user', 'admin'], default: 'user' \\
isVerified & Boolean & Default: true \\
profile.phone & String & Optional \\
profile.nidNumber & String & Optional \\
profile.dateOfBirth & Date & Optional \\
profile.gender & Enum & ['male', 'female', 'other'] \\
profile.occupation & String & Optional \\
profile.emergencyContact & Object & name and phone \\
profile.address & Object & street, city, district, postalCode \\
preferences & Object & User settings \\
createdAt, updatedAt & Timestamps & Auto-generated \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{User Model Schema}
\label{tab:user-schema}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Methods and Middleware}
\begin{itemize}
\item \code{comparePassword()}: Compares plain text password with hashed password
\item \textbf{Pre-save middleware}: Automatically hashes password before saving
\end{itemize}

