\hypertarget{mongodb-collections-and-schema}{%
\subsection{12. MONGODB COLLECTIONS AND
SCHEMA}\label{mongodb-collections-and-schema}}

\hypertarget{collection-initialization}{%
\subsubsection{12.1 Collection
Initialization}\label{collection-initialization}}

\textbf{Location}: Models are defined in server/models/ directory
\textbf{Initialization}: Mongoose automatically creates collections when
first document is inserted \textbf{Connection}: Established in
server/server.js using mongoose.connect()

\hypertarget{collection-details}{%
\subsubsection{12.2 Collection Details}\label{collection-details}}

\hypertarget{users-collection}{%
\paragraph{12.2.1 users Collection}\label{users-collection}}

\textbf{Schema File}: server/models/User.js \textbf{Purpose}: Store user
account information and profiles \textbf{Key Fields}: - \_id: MongoDB
ObjectId (auto-generated) - name: User's full name - email: Unique email
address - password: Hashed password - role: User role (user/admin) -
profile: Nested object with personal information - createdAt, updatedAt:
Timestamps

\textbf{Indexes}: - email: Unique index for fast lookup - createdAt: For
sorting and analytics

\hypertarget{services-collection}{%
\paragraph{12.2.2 services Collection}\label{services-collection}}

\textbf{Schema File}: server/models/Service.js \textbf{Purpose}: Store
property listing information \textbf{Key Fields}: - \_id: MongoDB
ObjectId (auto-generated) - title: Property title - propertyType: Type
of property - location: Nested object with address details - price:
Monthly rent amount - availability: Date range and status -
propertyDetails: Bedrooms, bathrooms, etc. - amenities: Available
facilities - owner: Reference to users collection - createdAt,
updatedAt: Timestamps

\textbf{Indexes}: - location.district: For location-based queries -
location.area: For area-specific searches - price: For price range
filtering - owner: For owner's listings

\hypertarget{bookings-collection}{%
\paragraph{12.2.3 bookings Collection}\label{bookings-collection}}

\textbf{Schema File}: server/models/Booking.js \textbf{Purpose}: Store
booking requests and status \textbf{Key Fields}: - \_id: MongoDB
ObjectId (auto-generated) - service: Reference to services collection -
user: Reference to users collection - startDate, endDate: Booking period
- totalAmount: Total booking cost - status: Booking status -
paymentStatus: Payment status - confirmationCode: Unique booking code -
createdAt, updatedAt: Timestamps

\textbf{Indexes}: - user: For user's booking history - service: For
property booking history - startDate, endDate: For availability checking

\hypertarget{payments-collection}{%
\paragraph{12.2.4 payments Collection}\label{payments-collection}}

\textbf{Schema File}: server/models/Payment.js \textbf{Purpose}: Store
payment transaction records \textbf{Key Fields}: - \_id: MongoDB
ObjectId (auto-generated) - booking: Reference to bookings collection -
user: Reference to users collection - service: Reference to services
collection - amount: Payment amount - paymentMethod: Method used for
payment - status: Payment status - paymentDetails: Method-specific
details - createdAt, updatedAt: Timestamps

\textbf{Indexes}: - user: For user's payment history - booking: For
booking-payment relationship - status: For status-based queries -
createdAt: For chronological sorting

\hypertarget{data-relationships}{%
\subsubsection{12.3 Data Relationships}\label{data-relationships}}

\textbf{Reference Relationships}: - User → Services (One-to-Many) - User
→ Bookings (One-to-Many) - User → Payments (One-to-Many) - Service →
Bookings (One-to-Many) - Booking → Payment (One-to-One)

\textbf{Population Usage}: - Services populated with owner information -
Bookings populated with user and service details - Payments populated
with booking and user information

\hypertarget{database-operations}{%
\subsubsection{12.4 Database Operations}\label{database-operations}}

\textbf{Common Operations}: - CRUD operations for all collections -
Complex queries with filtering and sorting - Aggregation pipelines for
analytics - Population for related data - Indexing for performance
optimization

\textbf{Performance Considerations}: - Proper indexing on frequently
queried fields - Pagination for large result sets - Efficient query
patterns - Connection pooling - Query optimization

================================================================================

\hypertarget{conclusion}{%
\subsection{CONCLUSION}\label{conclusion}}

This comprehensive analysis covers the complete architecture and
implementation of the NestUp BD platform. The system demonstrates a
well-structured full-stack application with proper separation of
concerns, secure authentication, robust data modeling, and user-friendly
interfaces. The modular design allows for easy maintenance and future
enhancements while providing a solid foundation for a property rental
platform.

The codebase follows modern web development best practices with React
for the frontend, Node.js/Express for the backend, and MongoDB for data
persistence. The authentication system ensures security, while the
payment system provides multiple options for users. The comprehensive
API design enables smooth communication between frontend and backend
components.

This documentation serves as a complete reference for understanding,
maintaining, and extending the NestUp BD platform.

